  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Stamp out Poverty</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Stamp Out Poverty is a network which is campaigning for two objectives &#x2013; 
1.	innovative sources of financing for development, particularly a stamp duty on transactions in and out of sterling, and
2.	the principle that monies raised in this way should be hypothecated for development.

Stamp Out Poverty has been successful in assembling high quality evidence to overcome inevitable objections from the Treasury and the City of London, but the Treasury predictably is proving a hard nut to crack.  Stamp Out Poverty has had more success internationally: the Leading Group of nations on innovative finance &#x2013; France, Brazil and Norway &#x2013; have all hosted international conferences on the issue with Stamp Out Poverty providing keynote speakers to each of them.

The first ever development tax, funded by a number of aviation levies, was launched in September 2006. Led by France, several countries are funding UNITAID &#x2013; an international drug purchasing facility for HIV/AIDS, TB and malaria treatments &#x2013; at a level of $300 million annually. Two months later, a UK-led scheme - the International Finance Facility for Immunisation &#x2013; was launched providing $4 billion for vaccinations over the next 10 years. Stamp Out Poverty played a significant role in opening up the political space for this important progress to happen.

Stamp Out Poverty plans to continue work on the principle of a stamp duty on sterling transactions and will work with other organisations and networks on other innovative sources of finance, including the additional levies and taxes needed to fund adaptation to climate change.</narrative>
  </description>
